WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.479
Fifty-four years after Disney brought Mary
Poppins to life on the big screen, the magical

00:00:05.479 --> 00:00:07.069
British nanny is back!

00:00:07.069 --> 00:00:11.900
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm comparing Mary Poppins Returns

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:17.770
to the 1964 classic, revealing the biggest
differences and whether it's a worthy sequel.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:22.539
When Julie Andrews made her film debut as
Mary Poppins over half a century ago, she

00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:26.630
lit up the screen with her take on author
PL Travers' no-nonsense nanny.

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:31.000
I'm sure it must be daunting for any actor
to step into Andrews' practically perfect

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.550
shoes, but if anyone's the right person to
slip on Mary Poppins' very particular footwear,

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:39.870
then it's Emily Blunt, who's repeatedly shown
what an incredibly versatile and meticulous

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.000
actress she is.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.710
This latest version of Mary Poppins isn't
exactly the same as the original movie and

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:49.010
it's certainly not an impersonation of Julie
Andrews either.

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:53.890
Blunt took her Poppins cues from the character
PL Travers created in her books, which means

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:57.910
she's somewhat sterner, sharper, and ruder
than in the first film.

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:01.859
And then, there's Mary's new accent, which
I wasn't sure about when I heard it in the

00:01:01.859 --> 00:01:02.859
trailer.

00:01:02.859 --> 00:01:06.520
But, pish-posh to that, quite frankly, as
when I saw the film, I've gotta say the accent

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.270
wasn't an issue for me.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:13.441
Yes, Blunt has gone many times posher than
her own Received Pronunciation, or Julie Andrews'

00:01:13.441 --> 00:01:17.440
for that matter, but her performance sold
her clipped, heightened tones, inspired by

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.689
British royalty, and made it work in the 1930s
context of the film.

00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:26.380
As for the song-and-dance numbers, Blunt looks
like she's having a total blast which makes

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.240
watching her a real joy.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.340
So, while she takes her Mary in a different
direction to Andrews, each actress is beguiling

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.240
in the role in her own way.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.549
And just seeing Mary Poppins come floating
down through the skies once more may well

00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:41.610
bring a tear to your eye.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:46.119
In the original Mary Poppins, Dick Van Dyke
was such an absolute ball of energy as the

00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:51.450
all-singing, all-dancing chimney sweep, street-artist,
and one-man-band Bert that he practically

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.110
burst off the screen.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:57.270
Though a new addition to the story, Lin-Manuel
Miranda's character Jack the lamplighter is

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:01.509
given a brief backstory woven in with the
original characters as it turns out that he

00:02:01.509 --> 00:02:03.689
used to be Bert's apprentice.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:08.149
Van Dyke's larger-than-life performance as
Bert is so memorable that it's a really tough

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:12.660
act to follow, but Miranda is an accomplished
musical performer and plays Jack with a good

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:14.010
deal of charm.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:19.900
In fact, Jack not only inherits Bert's child-like
wonder and optimism, but also his interesting

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:21.610
take on a Cockney accent!

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:23.760
"As I live and breathe!"

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.820
Miranda's attempt is a touch better than Van
Dyke's though he's helped by the fact that,

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:32.950
unlike the 1964 film, the sequel opens with
Jack singing rather than speaking, so the

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:35.630
faulty accent doesn't hit you quite as immediately.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:41.720
"Comical poems suitable for the occasion,
extemporised and thought up before your very

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:42.720
eyes."

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.370
Speaking of Dick Van Dyke, he makes a very
welcome return in the sequel!

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:50.960
This time, we're told that jack-of-all-trades
Bert is off travelling in 'parts unknown'.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:56.310
So, instead, Van Dyke makes a cameo as Mr
Dawes Jr, who also appeared in the first film,

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.200
though played by a different actor.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:03.630
In fact, Mr Dawes Jr is the son of former
Bank Chairman Mr Dawes Sr, who was the second

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:06.120
character Van Dyke played in the original
film!

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:11.200
And, let me tell you, it really is amazing
to see the 90-something-year-old actor singing

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:16.050
and dancing with incredible gusto once more
and, amazingly, he looks pretty much just

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:22.040
like he did when he appeared as Mr Dawes Sr
in full old-man make-up back in the 1960s!

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:27.790
With the sequel taking place around 25 years
after the original movie, the Banks children,

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.150
Michael and Jane, are now the adults of the
story.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:34.850
These days, Jane follows the activist path
of her mother, and Michael works for Fidelity

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:38.300
Fiduciary Bank, just like his father did before
him.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:42.880
Also, much like his dad, Michael's problems
and preoccupation with work mean he's lost

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.450
his child-like joy, and both films are about
getting that back and appreciating your family.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:52.630
While Michael does lose his temper with his
children, similar to his father, overall,

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:57.350
he's less stern and markedly gentler with
his kids, and also much less prim and proper

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:58.560
than his dad.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.840
Ben Whishaw brings a lovely, delicate touch
to the role of the grieving Michael and his

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:08.180
performance of the melancholy song, 'A Conversation',
proves a real tearjerker.

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:13.820
And Emily Mortimer is delightful as Jane,
mixing can-do spirit with moments of shy sweetness.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:19.250
So many of the songs composed by the Sherman
Brothers for the 1964 movie became well-loved

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:23.250
classics still remembered fondly by children
and adults today.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:28.870
Whether it's the Oscar-winning Chim Chim Cher-ee,
A Spoonful of Sugar, Supercalifragilisticexpialidocious,

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:33.750
Jolly Holiday, Let's Go Fly A Kite, or Feed
the Birds, if you're watching this video then

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:38.520
you likely know at least the tune if not a
good number or even all the lyrics of those

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:39.520
songs.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.300
Obviously, following the Sherman Brothers
is a tall order then.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.380
Still, Marc Shaiman and Scott Wittman, who
composed the musical Hairspray, have done

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:51.200
a top-notch job capturing the tone of the
original movie and recreating the kind of

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:53.650
tunes that reflect the style and era.

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:57.920
It's tough to say if the songs are as iconic
as the originals, given how many times we've

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:02.400
heard those in comparison, but many of them
are definitely very catchy and others certainly

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:03.430
grow on you.

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:07.740
My favourite song among the new batch is 'A
Cover Is Not The Book', which is a dazzling,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:12.840
humorously innuendo-laden vaudeville number
brought to rollicking life with spirited performances

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.560
by Emily Blunt and Lin-Manuel Miranda.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:20.160
As well as the fast-paced fun of 'Trip A Little
Light Fantastic', 'Can You Imagine That?',

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:24.260
and 'The Royal Doulton Music Hall', we also
get the delightfully lilting '(Underneath

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:29.680
The) Lovely London Sky' and the heartfelt
lullaby, 'The Place Where Lost Things Go'.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.081
And although the new cast don't perform any
of the songs from the original movie, you

00:05:33.081 --> 00:05:37.840
will hear traces of their melodies carefully
woven into the score at several suitable points

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:39.750
throughout the film.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:44.700
Mary Poppins Returns is a real love letter
to the 1964 movie made with obvious affection

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:46.170
by all involved.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:51.230
In fact, it reminds me in some ways of Star
Wars: The Force Awakens which likewise re-kick-started

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:54.230
a popular property after a long absence.

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:56.540
But, is it as original as the classic movie?

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:01.540
Well, no, in many ways it can't be because
it takes us back to a place we already know

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:06.880
and reminds us of that throughout with numerous
echoes of the original film in story beats,

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:08.940
characters, visuals, and songs.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:14.200
But director Rob Marshall and screenwriter
David Magee's nostalgia-filled film does manage

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:19.730
to put enough of its own twist on things and
finds added inspiration in PL Travers' various

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:21.690
Mary Poppins books.

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:27.070
Of course, there are so many wonderful moments
of magic in the 1960s movie, from Mary and

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:31.830
the children tidying the nursery, to Uncle
Albert's airborne tea party, to the Step In

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:36.670
Time roof-top dance sequence, and, of course,
who can forget the artful mix of live-action

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:41.240
and animation where Mary, Bert, and the children
jump inside a chalk drawing of the typical

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:43.090
English countryside?!

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:47.520
Mary Poppins Returns also has some appealing
magic scenes, including the children's bath

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:52.530
time which turns into an underwater fantasy
and the sequel's take on the Step In Time

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:56.830
scene is a fun reverse of the original where
the children go down in the world via a London

00:06:56.830 --> 00:06:59.389
lamp-post rather than up on the rooftops.

00:06:59.389 --> 00:07:04.400
Best of all though is the gorgeous Royal Doulton
china bowl sequence which features a raucous

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:08.370
music hall and mixes live-action and hand-drawn
animation.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:12.470
And I also really love the genius touch of
having the characters' clothes appear hand-painted!

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:18.410
In fact, Oscar-winning costume designer Sandy
Powell's work throughout this movie is simply

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:19.440
stunning.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.260
While the sequel does gift us special appearances
from Dick Van Dyke and Karen Dotrice, who

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:29.500
played Jane Banks in 1964, sadly, the original
Mary Poppins, Julie Andrews, doesn't appear

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:33.581
because, according to the movie's director,
she didn't want to distract from the sequel

00:07:33.581 --> 00:07:34.770
being "Emily's show".

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:40.010
However, we do get a charming cameo from another
screen legend, Angela Lansbury, who makes

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:44.430
an appearance as the Balloon Lady, a character
from PL Travers' books.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:48.880
Lansbury even gets to sing a song in her inimitable
style, and her presence in the film feels

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:53.910
practically perfect especially given her musical
history with Disney, starring in their live-action

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:58.100
film Bedknobs And Broomsticks and the animated
Beauty And The Beast where she voiced Mrs

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:01.750
Potts and memorably sang the title song.

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:05.910
Another new addition to the sequel is Meryl
Streep's character, Topsy, who, like Lansbury's

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:09.220
Balloon Lady, is adapted from PL Travers'
books.

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:13.970
This is Streep's third film with Emily Blunt,
following The Devil Wears Prada and Into The

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:18.639
Woods, and she's clearly having a ball totally
hamming it up with a deliberately OTT accent

00:08:18.639 --> 00:08:20.880
as Mary's eccentric cousin.

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:25.090
Streep's appearance in the movie includes
a deliciously jazzy showstopper called Turning

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:29.500
Turtle, and the whole scene feels like a fun
call-back with a twist to the first film's

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:31.670
laugh-filled, floating scene with Uncle Albert.

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:36.029
By the way, I've got a video about all the
sequel's easter eggs which I'll link to here

00:08:36.029 --> 00:08:37.849
and in the video description.

00:08:37.849 --> 00:08:43.390
At times, Mary Poppins Returns is a little
more melancholy than the original; after all,

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:47.190
Michael has lost his wife and the children
their mother, and they're about to be evicted

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:48.279
from their home.

00:08:48.279 --> 00:08:53.100
In that respect, it's similar to another recent
musical sequel, Mamma Mia: Here We Go Again,

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:56.170
which, coincidentally, also starred three
of this film's cast.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:09:00.700
Nevertheless, there's still plenty of uplifting,
cheery fun as well as hope and happiness to

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:02.600
be had with Emily Blunt's Mary.

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:07.320
Sure, bringing the magical British nanny back
on screen after over 50 years' absence is

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:13.260
no easy task as generation after generation
has grown up with Julie Andrews' classic performance

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:16.620
and watching that movie feels like getting
a big old hug.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:21.330
But, if you let yourself experience the sequel
with the kind of child-like wonder that Mary

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:26.260
Poppins advocates, then you'll likely enjoy
its genuine warmth and delightfully old-fashioned

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:27.260
appeal.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:29.390
It certainly put a big smile on my face!

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:32.860
And I'll happily watch it again and listen
to the soundtrack as well!

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:36.860
Now, how do you think Mary Poppins Returns
compares to the first film?

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:40.400
And what are your favourite moments or songs
in the new movie?

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:42.820
Let me know in the comments below.

